# Test: a peer waits for an in-tree plugin it attaches under an OPERATOR ALIAS.
#
# VALIDATES: RFC 4724 Section 4 -- "the End-of-RIB marker MUST be sent by a BGP
#            speaker to its peer once it completes the initial routing update
#            (including the case when there is no update to send) for an address
#            family after the BGP session is established". The barrier that
#            holds that marker is populated from the plugin's declaration
#            (registry.Registration.SignalsSessionReady), and the peer names its
#            processes by the name the OPERATOR chose, not by the name the
#            registry filed.
#
# PREVENTS: the declaration being looked up in the wrong namespace.
#           `plugin { internal ribout { use bgp-rib } }` runs the process as
#           "ribout" while the registration lives under "bgp-rib", so a lookup
#           by the process name found nothing and answered "declares nothing".
#           Every aliased binding of a declaring plugin was dropped from the
#           barrier, and the marker then claimed an initial routing update that
#           bgp-rib had not finished. The alias form is what the repository's
#           own scenarios use (82 `internal rs`, 86 `internal adj-rib-in`), so
#           the barrier was empty on nearly every peer that had one.
#
# HOW THE ASSERTIONS DISCRIMINATE. An EMPTY barrier says nothing at all:
# a dropped declaration is silent on this rail: nothing refuses it, nothing
# waits for it, and the marker goes out either way, because ze stopped holding
# its own End-of-RIB for a process that creates routes (owner ruling,
# 2026-09-18). The two assertions therefore read SignalAPIReady's own two
# outcomes (internal/component/bgp/reactor/peer.go):
#   "credited to this peer's barrier"
#                         -- the barrier was armed AND it NAMES the reporting
#                            process. bgp-rib is the only process attached here,
#                            so this line names it by construction, and it
#                            cannot be reached with an empty barrier.
#   "barrier does not name"
#                         -- armed, and the report was dropped. Rejected: that
#                            is the silent drop this contract exists to remove,
#                            and it would otherwise satisfy nothing above while
#                            looking like progress.
# Measured on 2026-09-02 with the alias resolution removed from
# (*Server).declaresSessionReady: this file FAILS with `stderr does not contain
# "credited to this peer's barrier"`, and the daemon logs bgp-rib's report
# arriving against an EMPTY barrier -- `plugin session ready from a process this
# peer's barrier does not name ... process=ribout barrier=[]`. With the
# resolution in place the same run credits the report and names the process.
#
# WHY THE ASSERTIONS ARE LOG LINES AND NOT AN ORDERED PAIR OF WIRE MESSAGES.
# The stronger form of this test would hold a route in the RIB before the
# session comes up, then assert the UPDATE at seq=1 and the marker at seq=2. That
# pins RFC 4724 Section 4 on the wire instead of in the daemon's log. It is not
# reachable in this scenario, and each reason below was read at its producer on
# 2026-09-02.
#
# bgp-rib puts NOTHING before the marker on a peer's FIRST session.
# (*RIBManager).collectPeerUpReplay (bgp/plugins/rib/rib_replay.go) returns nil
# while seenBefore is false, so the Adj-RIB-Out replay starts at the SECOND
# establishment of the same peer. This file establishes once by construction, and
# the routes a second establishment would replay are the ones the first session
# sent, of which there are none.
#
# No directive holds a peer down while another producer fills that RIB. ze-peer
# takes file, asn, bind, conn_map, await_eor, linger, silent, tcp_connections,
# open, update, timeout and env (internal/test/peer/expect.go), and not one of
# them delays the connection. `peer pause` acts on a session that is already up
# (api-peer-pause-resume.ci asserts that peer's End-of-RIB first), and the peer
# model carries no admin-down leaf that would hold the session in config.
#
# The one producer that CAN put a route in before a first establishment is the
# redistribute rail, with a static route in the config, and it is the wrong
# witness. redistribute-orchestrator does not declare SignalsSessionReady, so its
# route is not held behind this barrier: it can leave before or after the marker,
# and an ordered pair over it would pin a guarantee ze does not make.
#
# WHY THE ALIAS IS NOT THE PLUGIN NAME. "ribout" is an arbitrary operator word.
# A file that wrote `internal bgp-rib { use bgp-rib }` would pass on the broken
# build too, because the process name is then a registry key by coincidence.
#
# The debug level is set for one subsystem only: those three lines are Debug,
# because an armed barrier that completes is the ordinary case and says nothing
# an operator must act on. The timeout arm is the one that logs at Warn.
option=env:var=ze.log.bgp.routes:value=debug

# ze-peer expectations
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=20s
# ze's initial-sync End-of-RIB for ipv4/unicast, the legacy encoding RFC 7606
# Section 5.2 names: a completely empty UPDATE. It is sent after the barrier
# releases, which is what the stderr lines below pin.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER

# ze bgp configuration
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal ribout {
		use bgp-rib
	}
}
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 10.0.0.1
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		# The three facts Peer.initialUpdateReporters needs. The peer GRANTS the
		# route-push rail with `send [ update ]`, bgp-rib DECLARES
		# SignalsSessionReady, and `receive [ state ]` TELLS the process the
		# session came up, which is the event its report answers. Deleting any
		# one of them empties the barrier and reddens this file.
		attach process ribout {
			receive [ update state ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Process orchestration.
#
# No `expect=exit:code=0` here, deliberately. That directive puts the runner on
# the branch that WAITS for the daemon to exit by itself (runner_exec.go, the
# `rec.ExpectExitCode != nil && fgProc != nil` case), and nothing in this file
# asks the daemon to stop: there is no fixture process and no observer, only a
# peer that reads one message. Measured 2026-09-02: with the exit expectation
# this file consumed the whole 20s budget times the parallel headroom and was
# reported as a timeout, while the daemon had done its work in under a second.
# Without it the runner waits for the peer, stops the daemon, and judges the
# stderr lines over what it collected.
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=20s
expect=stderr:contains=credited to this peer's barrier
reject=stderr:pattern=barrier does not name
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
